OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026", "title": "Positivity of Serre's Intersection Multiplicity", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "193", "family_title": "Serre’s intersection-multiplicity conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove Serre's positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic. If two nonzero finitely generated modules over a regular local ring have tensor product of finite length and complementary dimensions, then their intersection multiplicity is strictly positive.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\chi^R(M,N)>0. \\) There is no restriction on characteristic or ramification, and neither module is required to lift to an unramified regular local ring.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((R,\\mathfrak m)\\) be a commutative Noetherian regular local ring of dimension \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M,N\\) be nonzero finitely generated \\(R\\)-modules satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{length}_R(M\\otimes_R N)&lt;\\infty,\n  \\qquad\n  \\dim_R M+\\dim_R N=d.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define Serre’s intersection multiplicity by\n  \\[\n  \\chi^R(M,N)\n  =\n  \\sum_{i=0}^{d}(-1)^i\n  \\operatorname{length}_R\\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(M,N).\n  \\]\n  The support condition makes every term finite, and regularity makes the higher Tor groups vanish.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\chi^R(M,N)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThere is no restriction on characteristic or ramification, and neither module is required to lift to an unramified regular local ring.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 8, it suffices to prove positivity for a complete domain pair\n\\[\nD=R/P,\\qquad E=R/Q\n\\]\nof positive complementary dimensions, over an algebraically closed residue field of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\).</p><p>Fix \\(\\sigma\\in G_D\\). Propositions 9 and 10 construct the parameter quotient \\(U\\) and its finite approximations. Proposition 11 constructs a supported asymptotic sequence over \\(A_E\\subseteq E'\\), whose ordinary Euler evaluation is \\(c_\\sigma\\) on every \\(G_E\\)-conjugate. Proposition 12 gives its strictly positive normalized evaluations \\(b_{\\sigma,\\gamma}\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 7 to this supported sequence. It equates the sums of ordinary and normalized evaluations over all \\(G_E\\)-conjugates. Therefore\n\\[\n|G_E|c_\\sigma\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma\\in G_E}b_{\\sigma,\\gamma}\n&gt;0.                                                    \\tag{5.18}\n\\]\nThis construction applies to every \\(\\sigma\\in G_D\\).</p><p>Finally, Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|G_D|a_Da_E\\chi^R(D,E)\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in G_D}c_\\sigma\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac1{|G_E|}\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in G_D}\\sum_{\\gamma\\in G_E}\nb_{\\sigma,\\gamma}\n&gt;0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBoth ranks and both group orders are positive. Thus\n\\[\n\\chi^R(D,E)&gt;0,\n\\]\nand the reduction proves the theorem.</p><p>Neither norm comparison requires a Galois action on \\(C_D\\) or \\(C_E\\), nor invariance of either normalized length under a twist.</p><p>As the paper notes in Section 1.3, Theorem 1 and Kurano–Roberts [16, Theorem 3.2] also give the following symbolic-power consequence: if \\(R\\) is ramified mixed-characteristic regular local and \\(P,Q\\) are prime with\n\\[\n\\sqrt{P+Q}=\\mathfrak m,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ht}P+\\operatorname{ht}Q=\\dim R,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nP\\cap Q^{(r)}\\subseteq\\mathfrak m^{r+1}\n\\quad(r\\geq1),\n\\qquad\nQ^{(r)}=Q^rR_Q\\cap R.\n\\]\nThis is a direct application of their positivity-to-symbolic-power theorem, not an additional ingredient in the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2, “Finite strict-transform intersection.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\chi^A(A/P,A/Q) \\geq e_{\\mathfrak m}(A/P)e_{\\mathfrak m}(A/Q), \\) with equality if and only if \\(\\dim T=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A,\\mathfrak m)\\) be a commutative Noetherian regular local ring with residue field \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P,Q\\) be prime ideals satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{length}_A A/(P+Q)&lt;\\infty,\n  \\qquad\n  \\dim(A/P)+\\dim(A/Q)=\\dim A.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(e_{\\mathfrak m}\\) for Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  T=\\operatorname{gr}(A/P)\n  \\otimes_{\\operatorname{gr}A}\n  \\operatorname{gr}(A/Q),\n  \\]\n  where all associated graded rings use the maximal-ideal filtrations.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\dim T\\leq 1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\chi^A(A/P,A/Q)\n\\geq\ne_{\\mathfrak m}(A/P)e_{\\mathfrak m}(A/Q),\n\\]\nwith equality if and only if \\(\\dim T=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\dim A=0\\), then \\(A=k\\), \\(P=Q=0\\), and both sides are \\(1\\), with \\(T=k\\).</p><p>Otherwise, let \\(\\widetilde X\\) be the blowup of\n\\[\nX=\\operatorname{Spec}A\n\\]\nat its closed point, and let \\(\\widetilde Y,\\widetilde Z\\) be the strict transforms of\n\\[\nY=\\operatorname{Spec}(A/P),\n\\qquad\nZ=\\operatorname{Spec}(A/Q).\n\\]\nBy [27, Lemma 3.5], the underlying set\n\\[\nS=\\widetilde Y\\cap\\widetilde Z\n\\]\nidentifies with that of \\(\\operatorname{Proj}T\\). Since \\(\\dim T\\leq1\\), it is a finite set of closed points of the exceptional divisor, empty exactly when \\(\\dim T=0\\).</p><p>Fulton’s blowup formula and Skalit’s finite-support calculation [27, Theorem 3.4 and proof of Proposition 3.10] give\n\\[\n\\chi^A(A/P,A/Q)\n-\ne_{\\mathfrak m}(A/P)e_{\\mathfrak m}(A/Q)\n=\n\\sum_{x\\in S}\n[\\kappa(x):k]\\,\n\\chi^{B_x}(M_x,N_x),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nB_x=\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde X,x},\n\\qquad\nM_x=\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde Y,x},\n\\qquad\nN_x=\\mathcal O_{\\widetilde Z,x}.\n\\]</p><p>Each residue degree is finite and positive because the exceptional divisor is projective over \\(k\\). The blowup \\(\\widetilde X\\) is regular [14, Proposition A.4(1)]. The modules \\(M_x,N_x\\) are nonzero and finite over \\(B_x\\), and their tensor product has finite length because their intersection is supported on \\(S\\).</p><p>The local domains \\(A/P,A/Q\\), as well as \\(A\\), are formally equidimensional and universally catenary. Thus [27, Lemma 3.8 and proof of Proposition 3.10] gives\n\\[\n\\dim M_x=\\dim(A/P),\n\\qquad\n\\dim N_x=\\dim(A/Q),\n\\qquad\n\\dim B_x=\\dim A.\n\\]\nTheorem 1 therefore makes every local intersection multiplicity in the sum strictly positive.</p><p>The asserted inequality follows. Equality holds exactly when \\(S\\) is empty, equivalently when \\(\\dim T=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Residue-field extension.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a complete Noetherian local ring \\((B',\\mathfrak b',\\overline{k})\\) and a flat local map \\(B\\to B'\\) such that \\( \\mathfrak bB'=\\mathfrak b'. \\) For every finite \\(B\\)-module \\(V\\) and every \\(a\\geq 1\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((B,\\mathfrak b,k)\\) be a complete Noetherian local ring.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\overline{k}\\) be an algebraic closure of \\(k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a complete Noetherian local ring\n\\((B',\\mathfrak b',\\overline{k})\\) and a flat local map \\(B\\to B'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathfrak bB'=\\mathfrak b'.\n\\]\nFor every finite \\(B\\)-module \\(V\\) and every \\(a\\geq 1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{B'}\n\\left(\n\\frac{V\\otimes_B B'}{(\\mathfrak b')^a(V\\otimes_B B')}\n\\right)\n=\n\\operatorname{length}_B(V/\\mathfrak b^aV).\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, module dimensions and finite lengths are preserved. If \\(B\\) is regular, then \\(B'\\) is regular. For finite \\(B\\)-modules \\(M,N\\) whose tensor product has finite length, every Tor length, and hence their intersection multiplicity when defined, is preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>In equal characteristic, a coefficient field gives a presentation\n\\[\nB=k[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]]/I.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nB'\n=\n\\overline{k}[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]]\n/\nI\\overline{k}[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]].\n\\]\nThe map of power-series rings is flat, by the local flatness criterion for the variables and the flat coefficient-field extension. Its base change to the quotient has the asserted residue field and maximal ideal.</p><p>In the remaining case the residue characteristic is a prime \\(p\\). Cohen structure supplies a presentation\n\\[\nB=V[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]]/I,\n\\]\nwhere \\(V\\) is a complete discrete valuation ring with uniformizer \\(p\\) and residue field \\(k\\). We construct a discrete valuation extension with unchanged uniformizer and residue field \\(\\overline{k}\\), without assuming that \\(k\\) is perfect.</p><p>Given a simple algebraic residue-field extension, lift its monic irreducible polynomial to \\(f\\in V[X]\\). The algebra \\(V[X]/(f)\\) is finite free over \\(V\\), its reduction modulo \\(p\\) is the desired field, and all its maximal ideals lie over \\((p)\\). It is therefore local with maximal ideal generated by \\(p\\). Since \\(p\\) is a nonzerodivisor, it is a one-dimensional regular local ring, hence a discrete valuation ring. This argument also applies to inseparable residue extensions.</p><p>Perform this construction along a tower of simple algebraic extensions exhausting \\(\\overline{k}\\), taking unions at limit stages. In every such union, a nonzero element is a unit times a nonnegative integral power of \\(p\\). Every nonzero ideal is generated by an element of least valuation, so the union is still a discrete valuation ring. Its completion \\(V'\\) has uniformizer \\(p\\) and residue field \\(\\overline{k}\\). The map \\(V\\to V'\\) is flat because \\(V'\\) is torsion-free over \\(V\\). Now take\n\\[\nB'\n=\nV'[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]]\n/\nIV'[[X_1,\\ldots,X_t]].\n\\]\nThe same local flatness criterion proves the required flatness.</p><p>For either construction, tensor a composition series of a finite-length \\(B\\)-module with \\(B'\\). Flatness preserves exactness, and every simple factor becomes \\(B'/\\mathfrak b'\\). This proves the length assertions and, applied to \\(V/\\mathfrak b^aV\\), the displayed identity. Equality of Hilbert–Samuel functions gives equality of module dimensions.</p><p>If \\(B\\) is regular of dimension \\(d\\), its \\(d\\) maximal-ideal generators generate \\(\\mathfrak b'\\), and \\(\\dim B'=d\\), so \\(B'\\) is regular. Finally, flat base change identifies the Tor modules after extension; their finite lengths are preserved by the preceding argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 2.5, “Normalized-length input,” together with Corollary 2.6, “Finite-length base change.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a \\(D\\)-algebra \\(C_D\\), carrying a compatible \\(\\Lambda\\)-action, such that restriction of scalars defines an additive nonnegative length \\( \\lambda_D=\\lambda_\\infty \\) on its modules annihilated by a power of …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((D,\\mathfrak d,k)\\) be a complete Noetherian local domain of dimension \\(h&gt;0\\), where \\(k\\) is perfect of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A regular normalization base is a finite, generically separable inclusion \\(A\\subseteq D\\), inducing the identity on residue fields, of the form\n  \\[\n  A=k[[t_1,\\ldots,t_h]]\n  \\quad\\text{if }\\operatorname{char}D=p,\n  \\]\n  or\n  \\[\n  A=W(k)[[t_2,\\ldots,t_h]],\n  \\qquad t_1=p,\n  \\quad\\text{if }\\operatorname{char}D=0.\n  \\]\n  Such a base exists.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix such a base and compatible \\(p\\)-power roots of its parameters. Put\n  \\[\n  A_n=A[t_1^{1/p^n},\\ldots,t_h^{1/p^n}],\n  \\qquad\n  \\Lambda=\\varinjlim_n A_n.\n  \\]\n  In mixed characteristic, let \\(A_\\infty\\) be the \\(p\\)-adic completion of \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a sequence \\(z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_h)\\), write\n  \\[\n  K(z;V)\n  \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n  H_i(z;V)\n  \\]\n  for the homological Koszul complex and its homology. Write\n  \\(z^v=(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_h^v)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}_A D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a \\(D\\)-algebra \\(C_D\\), carrying a compatible \\(\\Lambda\\)-action, such that restriction of scalars defines an additive nonnegative length\n\\[\n\\lambda_D=\\lambda_\\infty\n\\]\non its modules annihilated by a power of \\(\\mathfrak d\\). For every parameter system \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_h\\) of \\(D\\) and every \\(v\\geq1\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_D H_i(z^v;C_D)=0\n\\qquad(i&gt;0),                                           \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\lambda_D\\bigl(C_D/(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_h^v)C_D\\bigr)\n=\nv^h e((z_1,\\ldots,z_h),D).                            \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nThe right side is positive and finite. The algebra and length are independent of the parameter system \\(z\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\lambda_D\\bigl(C_D/(t_1^v,\\ldots,t_h^v)C_D\\bigr)\n=\nv^h r.                                               \\tag{2.6}\n\\]</p><p>The length is defined on underlying tower modules, without requiring a \\(C_D\\)-action on the relevant submodules or covers. It has the finite-stage, infimum, supremum, and zero-length limit properties stated in Lemmas 2.2 and 2.3 below.</p><p>For every finite-length \\(A\\)-module \\(V\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_D\\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(V,C_D)=0\n\\quad(i&gt;0),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_D(V\\otimes_A C_D)=r\\operatorname{length}_A V.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\lambda_D(C_D/\\mathfrak dC_D)&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies the regular normalization base, and Lemmas 2.2 and 2.3 supply the tower length and its limit properties.</p><p>First assume that \\(D\\) has characteristic \\(p\\), and put\n\\[\nC_D=D_{\\mathrm{perf}}\n=\n\\bigcup_n D^{1/p^n}\n\\]\ninside a fixed algebraic closure of its fraction field. Here\n\\(\\Lambda=A_{\\mathrm{perf}}\\). Write\n\\[\nK=\\operatorname{Frac}(A),\n\\qquad\nL=\\operatorname{Frac}(D),\n\\qquad\nK'=K_{\\mathrm{perf}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(q=p^n\\), put\n\\[\nB_q=\\Lambda\\otimes_{A^{1/q}}D^{1/q}.\n\\]\nFlatness of \\(\\Lambda\\) over \\(A^{1/q}\\) and torsion-freeness of \\(D^{1/q}\\) embed this tensor product into\n\\[\nK'\\otimes_{K^{1/q}}L^{1/q}.\n\\]\nGeneric separability makes the latter a field, by linear disjointness from a purely inseparable extension. Thus\n\\[\nB_q=\\Lambda D^{1/q}\\subseteq C_D,\n\\]\nand these subrings increase to \\(C_D\\).</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be any \\(\\mathfrak d\\)-primary ideal. Set\n\\[\nV_q=B_q/HB_q,\n\\qquad\nW_q=\\operatorname{im}(V_q\\to C_D/HC_D),\n\\]\nand write \\(H^{[q]}=(a^q:a\\in H)\\). Finite-stage normalization and the \\(q\\)-th-power isomorphism give\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\infty(V_q)\n=\nq^{-h}\\operatorname{length}_D(D/H^{[q]}).\n\\]</p><p>Although \\(B_q\\subseteq C_D\\), the map on these quotients need not be injective. We control its kernel by a single trace denominator. There is a nonzero \\(g\\in A\\) with\n\\[\ngC_D\\subseteq B_1,\n\\qquad\ng^{1/q}C_D\\subseteq B_q.                              \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nIndeed, choose a \\(K\\)-basis \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_r\\) of \\(L\\) in \\(D\\) and its trace-dual basis \\(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_r\\). A common denominator \\(g\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) makes every \\(g\\beta_j\\) lie in \\(D\\). The field \\(LK'\\) is perfect and equals \\(L_{\\mathrm{perf}}\\). The ring \\(\\Lambda\\) is normal, since an integral equation and the fractions occurring in it descend to a normal stage \\(A^{1/q}\\). For \\(c\\in C_D\\), each\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{LK'/K'}(g\\beta_jc)\n\\]\nis integral over \\(\\Lambda\\) and belongs to \\(K'\\), hence belongs to \\(\\Lambda\\). The trace-dual expansion of \\(gc\\) proves the first inclusion in (2.7). Taking \\(q\\)-th roots proves the second.</p><p>The kernel of \\(V_q\\to W_q\\) is killed by \\(g^{1/q}\\). Kernel and cokernel of an endomorphism of a finite-length object have equal length. Applied to multiplication by \\(g^{1/q}\\), this gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\infty\\ker(V_q\\to W_q)\n\\leq\nq^{-h}\\operatorname{length}_D D/(H^{[q]},g)\n=\nO(q^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor this estimate, if \\(H\\) has \\(s\\) generators, then\n\\[\nH^{s(q-1)+1}\\subseteq H^{[q]}.\n\\]\nThus \\(H^{[q]}\\) contains a power of \\(\\mathfrak d\\) with exponent \\(O(q)\\), whereas\n\\(\\dim D/(g)\\leq h-1\\) if \\(g\\) is a nonunit. Hilbert–Samuel growth gives the bound; for a unit \\(g\\), the quotient is zero.</p><p>The \\(W_q\\) increase to \\(C_D/HC_D\\) and are uniformly primary-torsion. Monsky’s Hilbert–Kunz existence theorem and Lemma 2.3 show\n\\[\n\\lambda_D(C_D/HC_D)=e_{\\mathrm{HK}}(H,D).              \\tag{2.8}\n\\]</p><p>For a parameter system \\(z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_h)\\), finite-stage flatness gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\infty H_i(z;B_q)\n=\nq^{-h}\n\\operatorname{length}_D H_i(z_1^q,\\ldots,z_h^q;D).\n\\]\nThe Koszul complex \\(K(z;D)\\) has finite free terms in degrees \\(0,\\ldots,h\\) and finite-length homology. Roberts’ Frobenius homology theorem, in the form [19, Theorem 2.4], says that this quantity tends to zero for \\(i&gt;0\\). Its hypotheses require a complete characteristic-\\(p\\) ring and a finite-free complex of length its dimension with finite-length homology.</p><p>Filtered colimits commute with homology, and these Koszul homology modules have the common annihilator \\((z)\\). Lemma 2.3 proves (2.4), also with \\(z\\) replaced by \\(z^v\\). Finally,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{h}(-1)^i\n\\operatorname{length}_D H_i(z_1^q,\\ldots,z_h^q;D)\n=\nq^h e((z),D).\n\\]\nAfter division by \\(q^h\\), the positive-degree terms tend to zero. Together with (2.8) and the multiplicity power rule, this proves (2.5).</p><p>Now suppose \\(D\\) has mixed characteristic. Define\n\\[\nC_D=(D\\otimes_A A_\\infty)_{\\mathrm{perfd}},\n\\]\nthe perfectoidization of [6, Notation 4.0.1], built on Bhatt–Scholze’s construction [4, Proposition 8.5]. The stated hypotheses there are that \\(D\\) is a complete mixed-characteristic local domain with perfect residue field and the chosen finite Witt power-series base \\(A\\).</p><p>Choose \\(g\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) so that\n\\[\nA[1/g]\\to D[1/g]\n\\]\nis finite étale. Corollary 4.0.4 of [6] annihilates positive Koszul homology for every parameter system of \\(A_n\\otimes_A D\\) by the almost ideal \\((g)_{\\mathrm{perfd}}\\). At \\(n=0\\), this includes every parameter system of \\(D\\). Proposition 4.0.10 of that paper makes these uniformly \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\)-power-torsion homology modules have normalized length zero. Proposition 4.0.14, again at \\(n=0\\), identifies the length of the parameter quotient with its Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity. Applied also to \\(z^v\\), these results prove (2.4) and (2.5) for this single algebra \\(C_D\\). Lemma 2.2 permits restriction to the uncompleted tower throughout.</p><p>In either characteristic, a module killed by a power of \\(\\mathfrak d\\) is killed by a power of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\), so the claimed lengths are defined. Additivity and the tower properties are those established above. The multiplicity rank formula and equality of the residue fields give\n\\[\ne((t),D)\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}_A D\\,e((t),A)\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}_A D,\n\\]\nproving (2.6).</p><p>For the finite-length base-change assertion, first take \\(V=k\\). The Koszul resolution over regular \\(A\\), together with (2.4) and (2.6), gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_D\\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(k,C_D)=0\n\\quad(i&gt;0),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_D(k\\otimes_A C_D)=r.\n\\]\nInduct on a composition series of \\(V\\). The Tor long exact sequences and length additivity discard all positive-degree terms and show that degree-zero length increases by \\(r\\) at each step. All modules have bounded primary torsion.</p><p>For positivity of the residue quotient, choose a parameter ideal \\(H\\) of \\(D\\). Tensor a composition series of \\(D/H\\) with \\(C_D\\). Right exactness gives a filtration of \\(C_D/HC_D\\) whose factors are quotients of \\(C_D/\\mathfrak dC_D\\). Therefore\n\\[\n0&lt;e(H,D)\n=\n\\lambda_D(C_D/HC_D)\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{length}_D(D/H)\n\\lambda_D(C_D/\\mathfrak dC_D).\n\\]\nThis proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “Regular normalization bases.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a finite, generically separable inclusion \\(A\\subseteq D\\), inducing the identity on residue fields, with \\( A=k[[t_1,\\ldots,t_h]] \\quad\\text{if }\\operatorname{char}D=p, \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((D,\\mathfrak d,k)\\) be as in Proposition 2, before choosing \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite, generically separable inclusion \\(A\\subseteq D\\), inducing the identity on residue fields, with\n\\[\nA=k[[t_1,\\ldots,t_h]]\n\\quad\\text{if }\\operatorname{char}D=p,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA=W(k)[[t_2,\\ldots,t_h]]\n\\quad\\text{if }\\operatorname{char}D=0.\n\\]\nIn the second case set \\(t_1=p\\). In both cases \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_h\\) is a parameter system of \\(D\\) and a regular parameter system of \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The positive-characteristic assertion is the Cohen–Gabber normalization theorem [17, Theorem 1.1].</p><p>In mixed characteristic, choose a coefficient ring \\(W(k)\\subseteq D\\) and extend \\(p\\) to a parameter system\n\\[\np,t_2,\\ldots,t_h.\n\\]\nCompleteness and the parameter condition make the resulting power-series map module finite. Its kernel is zero: the source and target have dimension \\(h\\), whereas a nonzero ideal of the regular local domain \\(W(k)[[t_2,\\ldots,t_h]]\\) lowers dimension. The fraction-field extension is separable in characteristic zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, “The tower and its length.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(A_n\\) is a complete regular local ring with residue field \\(k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a regular normalization base \\(A\\subseteq D\\) and the tower \\(A_n,\\Lambda\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules killed by some power of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(A_n\\) is a complete regular local ring with residue field \\(k\\). For \\(b\\geq a\\), the map \\(A_a\\to A_b\\) is finite free of rank\n\\[\np^{(b-a)h}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(A\\to A_n\\) has rank \\(p^{nh}\\).</p><p>There is a nonnegative additive function \\(\\lambda_\\infty\\) on the indicated category of \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules with the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A finitely presented module descends to a finite-length \\(A_n\\)-module \\(V_n\\), and\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_\\infty(V_n\\otimes_{A_n}\\Lambda)\n   =\n   p^{-nh}\\operatorname{length}_{A_n}V_n.             \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For a finitely generated module \\(V\\), its length is the infimum of the lengths of finitely presented modules in this category admitting a surjection onto \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For an arbitrary module \\(V\\),\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_\\infty(V)\n   =\n   \\sup\\{\n   \\lambda_\\infty(V_0):\n   V_0\\subseteq V\\text{ is finitely generated}\n   \\}.                                               \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]</p><p>The value on an arbitrary module may be infinite. In mixed characteristic, restriction from \\(A_\\infty\\) to \\(\\Lambda\\) gives the same length on modules killed by a fixed power of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In characteristic \\(p\\), the stages are power-series rings in the chosen roots. In mixed characteristic they are power-series rings over the complete discrete valuation ring \\(W(k)[p^{1/p^n}]\\). The monomials in the successive roots, with each exponent less than the relevant root degree, form bases for all transition extensions. This proves the assertions about regularity, ranks, and residue fields.</p><p>Finite presentations over the union descend to a stage. The additional relations expressing annihilation by a fixed power of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\) also descend after increasing that stage, because there are finitely many generators and relations to consider. The resulting finite-stage module has finite length. Formula (2.2) is unchanged on enlarging the stage, by flatness and the computed free ranks.</p><p>The infimum and supremum extensions, including their additivity and compatibility with this formula, are Faltings’ normalized-length formalism [9, Appendix 2], in the formulation of [6, Definition 2.2.1 and Propositions 2.2.3, 2.2.6(a)]. The same residue field implies that \\(A_n\\)-length and \\(A\\)-length agree on finite-length \\(A_n\\)-modules.</p><p>In mixed characteristic, \\(\\Lambda\\) is \\(p\\)-torsion-free, and the natural map induces\n\\[\n\\Lambda/p^a\\Lambda\\simeq A_\\infty/p^aA_\\infty\n\\qquad(a\\geq1).\n\\]\nA module killed by \\(\\mathfrak m_A^a\\) is killed by \\(p^a\\). Its submodules and finite generating sets are therefore identical over the two bases. In computing the infimum over finitely presented covers of such a module, one may replace a cover \\(V\\) by \\(V/\\mathfrak m_A^aV\\); this is still finitely presented. These covers also correspond over the identical quotient rings, proving the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, “Zero-length limits.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Sums, direct sums, and directed colimits of zero-length modules have length zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the tower length \\(\\lambda_\\infty\\) of Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose all modules under consideration are killed by one fixed power of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Sums, direct sums, and directed colimits of zero-length modules have length zero. For an increasing union,\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\infty\\left(\\bigcup_a V_a\\right)\n=\n\\sup_a\\lambda_\\infty(V_a).\n\\]\nIf \\((V_a)_{a\\geq1}\\) is a direct system with\n\\(\\lambda_\\infty(V_a)\\to0\\), then\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\infty(\\varinjlim_a V_a)=0,\n\\]\nwithout any injectivity assumption on the transition maps.</p>", "proof": "<p>A finite set of elements of a direct sum is contained in a finite subsum. Additivity and (2.3) give the assertion for direct sums; sums and directed colimits are quotients of direct sums.</p><p>For an increasing union, every finite generating set lies in one stage. Finally, a finitely generated submodule of \\(\\varinjlim_aV_a\\) is contained in the image of every sufficiently late \\(V_a\\), by lifting its generators to a common stage. Monotonicity bounds its length by \\(\\lambda_\\infty(V_a)\\) at all such stages. Apply (2.3). The common annihilator ensures throughout that these modules belong to the length category.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Theorem 3.1, “Supported rational descent.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Pullback induces an equivalence \\( K^J(H)_{\\mathbb Q} \\simeq \\bigl(K^J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)_{\\mathbb Q}\\bigr)^{hG}. \\) In particular …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a commutative Noetherian \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\)-algebra of finite Krull dimension.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\subseteq A\\) be an ideal containing \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a connective associative derived \\(A\\)-algebra with central \\(A\\)-action. Complexes are homologically indexed, so connectivity means vanishing of homotopy groups in negative degrees.</p><p class=\"li\">Perfect modules over associative algebras are right modules.</p><p class=\"li\">For a commutative \\(A\\)-algebra \\(B\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Perf}_J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}B)\n  \\]\n  be the category of perfect modules whose central localizations vanish off \\(V(JB)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(K^J\\) for its nonconnective algebraic \\(K\\)-theory and \\(K_0^J\\) for its zeroth homotopy group. A subscript \\(\\mathbb Q\\) denotes rationalization.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finite commutative \\(A\\)-algebra with an action of a finite group \\(G\\) over \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\operatorname{Spec}T\\to\\operatorname{Spec}A\\) is surjective and \\(G\\) acts transitively on the points of every geometric fiber.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\((-)^{hG}\\) for homotopy fixed points and \\((-)^G\\) for ordinary invariants.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Pullback induces an equivalence\n\\[\nK^J(H)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\simeq\n\\bigl(K^J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)_{\\mathbb Q}\\bigr)^{hG}.\n                                                               \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nK_0^J(H)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\longrightarrow\n\\bigl(K_0^J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\\bigr)^G\n                                                               \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nis surjective, in fact an isomorphism.</p><p>Neither flatness nor tame ramification of \\(A\\to T\\) is required. The algebra \\(H\\) need not be commutative, finite over \\(A\\), or bounded in positive homological degrees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nX=\\operatorname{Spec}T,\n\\qquad\nS=\\operatorname{Spec}A.\n\\]\nFor finite-type \\(S\\)-schemes consider the natural comparison\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(Y)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathcal L(X\\times_S Y)^{hG}.                          \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nBoth sides are sheaves for the topology used in Lemma 3.3: base change preserves the cover families, and homotopy fixed points commute with limits.</p><p>Applying finite-surjection descent to \\(X\\to S\\), it suffices to prove (3.7) on the nonaugmented terms of its Čech nerve. Over each such term, the pulled-back \\(X\\)-cover admits a section, given by a projection to one of its \\(X\\)-coordinates. Geometric transitivity is stable under base change. We are reduced to covers admitting a section.</p><p>We prove this case by induction on \\(\\dim Y\\). Nilinvariance and the closed-union squares in Lemma 3.3 reduce the base to an integral reduced scheme. Both sides of (3.7) have these descent properties; the component reduction uses finitely many closed pieces and lower-dimensional intersections.</p><p>Let\n\\[\ns:Y\\to X\\times_S Y\n\\]\nbe a section. Its finitely many \\(G\\)-translates cover the source on points: extend the residue field at any point of \\(Y\\) to an algebraic closure and use transitivity on that geometric fiber. Each translate is a closed immersion, since the source is separated over \\(Y\\), and each factors through\n\\[\nV=(X\\times_S Y)_{\\mathrm{red}}.\n\\]</p><p>Retain the distinct translated sections and index them by a transitive finite \\(G\\)-set \\(I\\). Two distinct sections have a proper closed coincidence locus in \\(Y\\): their equalizer is closed, and agreement at the generic point of integral reduced \\(Y\\) would imply agreement everywhere. Let \\(Z\\subsetneq Y\\) be the reduced union of these coincidence loci, with \\(Z=\\varnothing\\) if there is only one section.</p><p>The map\n\\[\nq:W=\\coprod_{i\\in I}Y\\longrightarrow V\n\\]\nis finite and surjective. Over \\(Y\\setminus Z\\), the section images are pairwise disjoint and cover the reduced scheme \\(V\\). Their disjoint union is therefore isomorphic to \\(V\\) there. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\begin{matrix}\nW\\times_Y Z&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;W\\\\\n\\downarrow&amp;&amp;\\downarrow\\\\\nV\\times_Y Z&amp;\\longrightarrow&amp;V\n\\end{matrix}\n\\]\nis a \\(G\\)-equivariant abstract blowup square.</p><p>The \\(G\\)-action on \\(W\\) merely permutes its copies of \\(Y\\). Restriction gives a \\(G\\)-equivariant equivalence\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(W)\\simeq\\prod_{i\\in I}\\mathcal L(Y).\n\\]\nA stabilizer of one section acts identically on that copy, including on the coefficient algebra pulled back from \\(Y\\). Thus this is the ordinary permutation action, with coherently trivial stabilizer action. The same holds over \\(Z\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) is rational, averaging over a finite group is exact. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(W)^{hG}\\simeq\\mathcal L(Y),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L(W\\times_Y Z)^{hG}\\simeq\\mathcal L(Z).         \\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nFor example, if \\(I=G/G_s\\), the first fixed-point spectrum is\n\\(\\mathcal L(Y)^{hG_s}\\) for the trivial action and is \\(\\mathcal L(Y)\\). This holds without boundedness: the trivial \\(\\mathbb Q[G_s]\\)-module \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is projective, so derived invariants are ordinary invariants even on unbounded rational complexes.</p><p>Apply \\(\\mathcal L\\) to the abstract blowup square and take homotopy \\(G\\)-invariants. The inductive hypothesis over \\(Z\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(V\\times_Y Z)^{hG}\\simeq\\mathcal L(Z).\n\\]\nNilinvariance allows passage between \\(V\\times_Y Z\\) and the corresponding reduced or unreduced pullback of the original cover. Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(V)^{hG}\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal L(Y)\\times_{\\mathcal L(Z)}\\mathcal L(Z)\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal L(Y).\n\\]\nNilinvariance restores \\(X\\times_S Y\\) in place of \\(V\\).</p><p>In dimension zero there are no proper nonempty coincidence loci, and the proof is the calculation for a disjoint union of section copies. This starts the induction.</p><p>Taking \\(Y=S\\) and using Lemma 3.1 proves (3.1). Finally, exact rational invariants give\n\\[\n\\pi_0\n\\left(\nK^J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)_{\\mathbb Q}^{hG}\n\\right)\n=\n\\bigl(\nK_0^J(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\bigr)^G.\n\\]\nThis proves (3.2), in fact as an isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "the construction in Section 3.1, “The truncating invariant and its supports.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The invariant \\(E\\) is truncating and localizing.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,J,H\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">For a derived \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\)-algebra \\(U\\), define\n  \\[\n  E(U)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{fib}\n  \\bigl(K(U)_{\\mathbb Q}\\to K(U[1/p])_{\\mathbb Q}\\bigr).\n                                                               \\tag{3.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite-type \\(A\\)-scheme \\(Y\\), let \\(H_Y\\) be the derived pullback of \\(H\\), and put\n  \\[\n  Z_Y=Y\\times_A V(J).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L(Y)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{fib}\n  \\bigl(E(Y;H)\\to E(Y\\setminus Z_Y;H)\\bigr),                     \\tag{3.5}\n  \\]\n  where coefficients mean evaluation on perfect \\(H_Y\\)-modules.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The invariant \\(E\\) is truncating and localizing. The functor \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a nilinvariant cdh sheaf, and\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(Y)\n\\simeq\nK\\bigl(\\operatorname{Perf}_{Z_Y}(H_Y)\\bigr)_{\\mathbb Q}.          \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nFor a perfect coefficient module \\(P\\), central-localization support is equivalent to the condition that a power of each generator of \\(J\\) kills \\(\\operatorname{id}_P\\) in the homotopy category.</p>", "proof": "<p>A localizing invariant takes exact sequences of stable categories to fiber sequences. It is truncating if its value on a connective associative algebra is unchanged by passage to \\(\\pi_0\\).</p><p>Rational infinitesimal \\(K\\)-theory\n\\[\nI(U)\n=\n\\operatorname{fib}\n\\bigl(\nK(U)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\to\nHN(U\\otimes\\mathbb Q/\\mathbb Q)\n\\bigr)\n\\]\nis truncating by Goodwillie’s relative comparison theorem [12], with passage from simplicial rings to connective associative ring spectra as explained in [18, proof of Corollary 3.9 and Remark 3.10]. Truncating localizing invariants satisfy cdh descent, also after tensoring with a fixed connective associative algebra over the base [18, Theorem A.2 and Appendix B, especially Theorem B.3].</p><p>The negative cyclic homology terms in the character maps for \\(U\\) and \\(U[1/p]\\) are naturally identical, since\n\\[\nU\\otimes\\mathbb Q\\simeq U[1/p]\\otimes\\mathbb Q.\n\\]\nTaking fibers of the character square gives\n\\[\nE(U)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{fib}\\bigl(I(U)\\to I(U[1/p])\\bigr).                \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nThus \\(E\\) is a truncating localizing invariant of \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\)-linear categories. This does not identify \\(K(U)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) with \\(K(U\\otimes\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p>Tensoring with \\(H\\) preserves the localizing property. It preserves truncation as well: for connective \\(B\\),\n\\[\nH\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}B\n\\]\nis connective, and\n\\[\n\\pi_0(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}B)\n=\n\\pi_0(H)\\otimes_A\\pi_0(B).\n\\]\nReplacing \\(B\\) by \\(\\pi_0(B)\\) therefore does not change the value of \\(E\\).</p><p>The coefficient theorem gives cdh descent for \\(E(-;H)\\). Restriction to the open complement of \\(Z_Y\\) preserves the squares defining the cdh topology, and limits commute with limits. Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies cdh descent.</p><p>Because \\(p\\in J\\), the support \\(Z_Y\\) disappears after inverting \\(p\\). Expanding the two fibers defining \\(\\mathcal L\\), and using perfect-complex localization, identifies \\(\\mathcal L\\) with the actual rational supported \\(K\\)-theory in (3.6), not a cdh replacement of it. The functor is nilinvariant as well.</p><p>For the support criterion, if \\(P\\) is perfect and \\(z\\in A\\), compactness identifies localization of its endomorphism spectrum with the endomorphism spectrum of \\(P[1/z]\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nP[1/z]=0\n\\]\nif and only if some power \\(z^v\\) kills \\(\\operatorname{id}_P\\) in the homotopy category. Apply this to finitely many generators of \\(J\\). The corresponding Koszul tensors generate the compact kernel of restriction to the complement. Perfect localization, including idempotent completion, computes the fiber in (3.6).</p><p>The geometric localization input is Thomason–Trobaugh’s perfect localization theorem [29, Propositions 5.2.2–5.2.4 and Theorem 7.4]. Tensoring its exact sequence with the perfect coefficient category, using [18, Lemmas 3.12 and B.2] and the supported-kernel identification, gives the coefficient version. Nonconnective \\(K\\)-theory sends this exact sequence to a fiber sequence by [5].</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The functor \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies ordinary Čech descent for \\(f\\), universally under base change.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be the functor of Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:X\\to Y\\) be a finite faithfully flat map of finite-type \\(A\\)-schemes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functor \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies ordinary Čech descent for \\(f\\), universally under base change.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since the schemes are Noetherian, \\(f_*\\mathcal O_X\\) is a vector bundle of positive rank. Restriction of scalars preserves perfect coefficient modules and their supports: locally, the scalar-extended coefficient algebra is a finite projective module over the original one. It therefore defines a transfer.</p><p>Finite-flat base change and the projection formula give natural transformations of presheaves on \\(Y\\)-schemes\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\n\\xrightarrow{f^*}\n\\mathcal L_X\n\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{tr}_f}\n\\mathcal L,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_X(W)=\\mathcal L(W\\times_YX),\n\\]\nwhose composite is tensoring with the pullback of \\(f_*\\mathcal O_X\\).</p><p>Work first on a Zariski open of \\(Y\\) on which that vector bundle is free of rank \\(a&gt;0\\), and fix a trivialization. Additivity of \\(K\\)-theory identifies the composite with multiplication by \\(a\\). The same trivialization pulls back on every \\(Y\\)-scheme, so the identity is natural on the whole presheaf diagram. Rationally, \\(a^{-1}\\operatorname{tr}_f\\) makes \\(\\mathcal L\\) a retract of \\(\\mathcal L_X\\).</p><p>After base change to \\(X\\), the augmented Čech nerve of \\(f\\) has a section and hence an extra degeneracy. Its image under any presheaf has split descent. Thus \\(\\mathcal L_X\\) satisfies descent for \\(f\\). The descent comparison for \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a retract of this equivalence, and is itself an equivalence.</p><p>Finally, both its source and its totalization are Zariski sheaves. The conclusion glues over the trivializing cover of \\(Y\\). All constructions commute with base change.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The functor \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies ordinary Čech descent for \\(X\\to Y\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be the functor of Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\to Y\\) be a finite surjection of finite-type \\(A\\)-schemes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functor \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies ordinary Čech descent for \\(X\\to Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\tau\\) be the topology generated by cdh covers and finite faithfully flat covers. These cover families are stable under base change. By Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2, \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a \\(\\tau\\)-sheaf. We show that every finite surjection \\(X\\to Y\\) is a \\(\\tau\\)-cover.</p><p>Reductions and finite closed decompositions are cdh-local operations. If a reduced scheme is a union\n\\[\nY=Y_1\\cup Y_2\n\\]\nof reduced closed subschemes, the proper map \\(Y_1\\to Y\\) is an isomorphism off \\(Y_2\\) and gives an abstract blowup square with intersection \\(Y_1\\cap Y_2\\). This reduces closed unions to their pieces. We may reduce to integral \\(Y\\), with induction on dimension for proper closed subsets.</p><p>Choose a reduced irreducible component \\(X_0\\) of \\(X\\) dominating \\(Y\\). The map \\(X_0\\to Y\\) is finite and surjective. On a dense open \\(U\\subseteq Y\\), it is finite flat of positive rank. Flattening by blowup, applied to \\(\\mathcal O_{X_0}\\), gives a blowup\n\\[\nY'\\to Y,\n\\]\nan isomorphism over \\(U\\), for which the strict transform\n\\[\nX_0'\\to Y'\n\\]\nis flat and finitely presented [28, Tag 0815]. The strict transform is a closed subscheme of \\(X_0\\times_Y Y'\\), so it is finite over \\(Y'\\). The blowup \\(Y'\\) is integral, and the image of \\(X_0'\\) contains its generic point. Finiteness makes that image closed. Thus \\(X_0'\\to Y'\\) is surjective and is a finite faithfully flat cover factoring through \\(X\\times_Y Y'\\).</p><p>Let \\(Z\\subsetneq Y\\) be the blowup center. The family\n\\[\n\\{Y'\\to Y,\\ Z\\to Y\\}\n\\]\nis a cdh cover. By induction on dimension,\n\\[\nX\\times_Y Z\\to Z\n\\]\nis a \\(\\tau\\)-cover. Together with \\(X_0'\\to Y'\\), these maps give a \\(\\tau\\)-covering family of \\(Y\\) factoring through \\(X\\). Hence \\(X\\to Y\\) generates a covering sieve.</p><p>The induction starts in dimension zero, where a reduced integral base is a field and any nonzero finite algebra is finite faithfully flat. This proves the assertion and the required Čech descent. The argument uses retracts and sheaf limits; it requires neither hyperdescent nor convergence of an unbounded descent spectral sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, “Ordinary norm comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a perfect \\(T\\)-complex \\(P\\) supported on \\(V(\\mathfrak m_AT)\\), the quantities \\( \\chi_T(P) = \\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{length}_T H_i(P), \\) and \\( \\chi_C(P) = \\sum_i(-1)^i\\lambda H_i(P\\otimes_T^{\\mathbf L}C) \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A,\\mathfrak m_A,k)\\) be a complete regular local \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\)-algebra of dimension \\(e&gt;0\\), with residue characteristic \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_e\\) be regular parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finite local domain extension of \\(A\\) with the same residue field, and put \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}_A T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a \\(T\\)-algebra equipped with an additive nonnegative length \\(\\lambda\\) on \\(C\\)-modules annihilated by powers of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\), with \\(\\lambda(0)=0\\). Infinite length is allowed for general modules.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume, for every \\(v\\geq1\\),\n  \\[\n  \\lambda H_i(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v;C)=0\n  \\quad(i&gt;0),\n  \\qquad\n  \\lambda\\bigl(C/(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v)C\\bigr)=v^er.       \\tag{3.9}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let a finite group \\(G\\) act on \\(T\\) over \\(A\\), transitively on every geometric fiber of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}T\\to\\operatorname{Spec}A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(g\\in G\\), define scalar twisting by\n  \\[\n  g^*P=P\\otimes_{T,g}T.\n  \\]\n  On a free complex, this applies \\(g\\) to every differential matrix entry.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a perfect \\(T\\)-complex \\(P\\) supported on \\(V(\\mathfrak m_AT)\\), the quantities\n\\[\n\\chi_T(P)\n=\n\\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{length}_T H_i(P),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\chi_C(P)\n=\n\\sum_i(-1)^i\\lambda H_i(P\\otimes_T^{\\mathbf L}C)\n\\]\nare finite and define homomorphisms on \\(K_0^{\\mathfrak m_A}(T)\\).</p><p>They agree on the image of \\(K_0^{\\mathfrak m_A}(A)\\), on every \\(G\\)-invariant rational class, and on every norm sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_T(g^*P)\n=\n\\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_C(g^*P).                             \\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nNo invariance of \\(\\lambda\\) under \\(G\\) is asserted or needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Represent \\(P\\) by a bounded finite free \\(T\\)-complex. Its ordinary homology is finite and supported at the closed point, hence has finite length.</p><p>For the \\(C\\)-evaluation, choose a common \\(v\\) such that each \\(z_j^v\\) acts nullhomotopically on \\(P\\), using the support criterion from Proposition 3. Put\n\\[\nK_v=K(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v;T).\n\\]\nSuccessively splitting the cones of these nullhomotopic actions gives\n\\[\nK_v\\otimes_T P\\otimes_T C\n\\simeq\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{e}\n(P\\otimes_T C)[j]^{\\oplus\\binom ej}.                   \\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nThe homotopies may be tensored with \\(C\\) and with the other Koszul factors, so the splitting respects the module structure on which \\(\\lambda\\) is defined.</p><p>Taking Koszul homology first gives a finite spectral sequence whose terms are finite direct sums of\n\\[\nH_j(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v;C).\n\\]\nTheir lengths are finite by (3.9). Every homology module on the left of (3.12) therefore has finite length. The splitting and nonnegativity give finiteness for every \\(H_i(P\\otimes_T C)\\). All homology modules and spectral-sequence terms to which \\(\\lambda\\) is applied are annihilated by fixed powers of \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\). The Euler sums are defined, and the long exact homology sequence of a triangle proves additivity.</p><p>Let \\(Q\\) be a perfect \\(A\\)-complex supported at \\(\\mathfrak m_A\\). Its homology has finite length. The finite hyper-Tor spectral sequence for \\(Q\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}C\\), together with Lemma 4.1, gives\n\\[\n\\chi_C(Q\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)\n=\nr\\sum_j(-1)^j\\operatorname{length}_A H_j(Q).           \\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nEvery positive Tor row has length zero, and the zeroth row has the displayed scaled lengths.</p><p>For the ordinary evaluation, choose a bounded finite free \\(A\\)-resolution \\(L_\\bullet\\) of \\(T\\), possible because \\(A\\) is regular. Its alternating rank is \\(r\\), as seen after tensoring with \\(\\operatorname{Frac}(A)\\). For a finite-length \\(A\\)-module \\(V\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{length}_A\\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(V,T)\n&amp;=\n\\sum_i(-1)^i\\operatorname{rank}_A(L_i)\\operatorname{length}_A V\\\\\n&amp;=\nr\\operatorname{length}_A V.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe hyper-Tor spectral sequence gives\n\\[\n\\chi_T(Q\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)\n=\nr\\sum_j(-1)^j\\operatorname{length}_A H_j(Q).           \\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nFinite-length \\(T\\)-modules have equal \\(A\\)-length and \\(T\\)-length because the residue fields agree. Thus the evaluations agree on pullbacks from \\(A\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 3 with \\(H=A\\) and \\(J=\\mathfrak m_A\\). Every invariant class in\n\\[\nK_0^{\\mathfrak m_A}(T)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\]\nis pulled back from a rational class downstairs. The two Euler homomorphisms therefore agree on that invariant subspace. The norm class\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in G}[g^*P]\n\\]\nis invariant, proving (3.11).</p><p>As emphasized in original Remark 3.6, the equality is for the sum of the conjugate classes, not for each individual class. This allows the two length algebras used later to be chosen independently.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(V,C)=0 \\quad(i>0), \\qquad \\lambda(V\\otimes_A C)=r\\operatorname{length}_A V. \\) All these lengths are finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,T,C,\\lambda,r\\) and (3.9) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a finite-length \\(A\\)-module.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(V,C)=0\n\\quad(i&gt;0),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda(V\\otimes_A C)=r\\operatorname{length}_A V.        \\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nAll these lengths are finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Koszul resolution on \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_e\\) resolves \\(k\\) over \\(A\\), so (3.9) with \\(v=1\\) proves the assertion for \\(V=k\\).</p><p>Induct on the length of \\(V\\) using a composition series. In the long exact Tor sequence associated to a short exact sequence with quotient \\(k\\), nonnegativity and additivity show that every positive Tor term has length zero. The image of the connecting map into degree zero also has length zero. Additivity in degree zero gives the factor \\(r\\). This simultaneously proves finiteness at every step.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are homological functors \\( H_i^{\\mathrm{ord}}: \\mathcal P_T^J\\to\\overline{\\mathcal A}_T, \\qquad H_i^C: \\mathcal P_T^J\\to\\overline{\\mathcal A}_C \\) whose values on a uniformly supported lift are its ordinary …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((A,J)\\) be an excellent regular local \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\)-algebra of dimension \\(e&gt;0\\), with residue characteristic \\(p\\), where \\(J\\) is the maximal ideal.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_e\\) be regular parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finite local domain over \\(A\\) with the same residue field, and put \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}_A T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a \\(T\\)-algebra with a nonnegative additive length \\(\\lambda\\) on modules annihilated by powers of \\(J\\). Assume\n  \\[\n  \\lambda H_j(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v;C)=0\n  \\quad(j&gt;0),\n  \\qquad\n  \\lambda\\bigl(C/(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v)C\\bigr)=v^er\n  \\quad(v\\geq1).                                      \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix positive integers \\(s_n\\) and a nonprincipal ultrafilter \\(\\mathcal U\\) on the positive integers. The estimates \\(O(s_n)\\) and \\(o(s_n)\\) refer to the ordinary limit.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S=A\\) or \\(T\\), take the additive category of sequences \\((S^{a_n})_n\\) with \\(a_n=O(s_n)\\), with arbitrary sequences of matrices as morphisms. Let \\(\\mathcal D_S\\) be its perfect hull: bounded complexes in one common degree range, followed by idempotent completion.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal N_S\\) be the thick subcategory generated by free sequences with \\(a_n=o(s_n)\\). Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_S=(\\mathcal D_S/\\mathcal N_S)^\\natural,\n  \\]\n  and write \\(q_S\\) for the quotient functor.</p><p class=\"li\">An object is supported on \\(J\\) if some common \\(v\\geq1\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  z_j^v\\operatorname{id}_X=0\n  \\quad\\text{for every }j\n  \\]\n  in the category containing \\(X\\). Let \\(\\mathcal S_S\\subseteq\\mathcal D_S\\) and\n  \\(\\mathcal P_S^J\\subseteq\\mathcal P_S\\) be the supported subcategories.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A_T\\) be the abelian category of sequences of finite \\(T\\)-modules annihilated by one common power of \\(J\\), with lengths \\(O(s_n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A_C\\) be the category of sequences of \\(C\\)-modules with the same uniform torsion condition and finite \\(\\lambda\\)-lengths \\(O(s_n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In either category, quotient by the Serre subcategory of sequences whose normalized ultralimit length is zero. Denote the quotients by\n  \\(\\overline{\\mathcal A}_T,\\overline{\\mathcal A}_C\\), and their finite nonnegative additive lengths by\n  \\(\\overline{\\operatorname{length}}_T,\\overline{\\lambda}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(K_v=K(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_e^v;A)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are homological functors\n\\[\nH_i^{\\mathrm{ord}}:\n\\mathcal P_T^J\\to\\overline{\\mathcal A}_T,\n\\qquad\nH_i^C:\n\\mathcal P_T^J\\to\\overline{\\mathcal A}_C\n\\]\nwhose values on a uniformly supported lift are its ordinary homology sequence and its homology sequence after tensoring with \\(C\\), respectively.</p><p>For each object \\(X\\), the numbers\n\\[\nb_i^{\\mathrm{ord}}(X)\n=\n\\overline{\\operatorname{length}}_T H_i^{\\mathrm{ord}}(X),\n\\qquad\nb_i^C(X)\n=\n\\overline{\\lambda}H_i^C(X)\n\\]\nare finite and nonnegative, and vanish outside a finite range. Their Euler sums define homomorphisms\n\\[\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}},\\chi_C:\nK_0(\\mathcal P_T^J)\\to\\mathbb R.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(F=(F_n)\\) is a bounded free sequence whose image is supported, and \\(v\\) makes all \\(z_j^v\\) act by zero on that image, then\n\\[\n(1+t)^e\\sum_i b_i(F)t^i\n=\n\\sum_i t^i\n\\lim_{n\\to\\mathcal U}\n\\frac{\\operatorname{len}H_i(K_v\\otimes_A F_n)}{s_n}.   \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nIn the ordinary case \\(\\operatorname{len}=\\operatorname{length}_T\\). In the \\(C\\)-case the complex on the right is further tensored over \\(T\\) with \\(C\\), and \\(\\operatorname{len}=\\lambda\\). This is an identity of finite Laurent polynomials.</p>", "proof": "<p>The support subcategories are thick: nilpotence of central actions is preserved by shifts, cones, and retracts, allowing larger powers for cones. The module-sequence categories in the statement are abelian because kernels, cokernels, and extensions preserve uniform torsion and the \\(O(s_n)\\) length bound. Zero normalized ultralimit length defines a Serre subcategory.</p><p>Begin with a bounded free sequence \\(F\\in\\mathcal S_T\\). Choose uniform powers annihilating its identity. Splitting their cones gives\n\\[\nK_v\\otimes_A F\n\\simeq\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{e}F[j]^{\\oplus\\binom ej}.              \\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>For ordinary length, every \\(H_j(K_v\\otimes_A T)\\) has finite length. Taking Koszul homology first bounds the homology lengths of \\(K_v\\otimes_A F_n\\) by fixed constants times the ranks of the terms of \\(F_n\\), hence by \\(O(s_n)\\).</p><p>For \\(C\\), the same spectral sequence uses (4.1): positive Koszul rows have length zero, and the zero row has finite length. Additivity and nonnegativity give the same bound. The splitting (4.3) bounds the homologies of \\(F_n\\) themselves. They have a common primary annihilator because a power of each \\(z_j\\) annihilates them.</p><p>A general \\(X\\in\\mathcal S_T\\) may be a retract in \\(\\mathcal D_T\\) of a bounded free sequence \\(F\\) that is not supported. Choose \\(v\\) killing the parameter actions on \\(X\\). Then \\(X\\) is a retract of \\(K_v\\otimes_A X\\), which is a retract of the uniformly supported bounded free sequence \\(K_v\\otimes_A F\\). The bounds apply to this sequence and hence to the homology summands defining \\(X\\). This is a retract before taking the rank quotient.</p><p>Fix \\(v\\). Koszul tensoring a rank-small free generator has homology of normalized length zero by the same bounds. The class of objects \\(Y\\in\\mathcal D_T\\) with this property for \\(K_v\\otimes_A Y\\) is thick, by homology long exact sequences, nonnegativity, and retracts. It contains \\(\\mathcal N_T\\). If\n\\[\nX\\in\\mathcal S_T\\cap\\mathcal N_T,\n\\]\nchoose \\(v\\) killing its parameter actions. Since \\(X\\) is a retract of \\(K_v\\otimes_A X\\), its homology is zero in both length quotients.</p><p>The homology functors therefore invert maps whose cones are supported and small. They descend to\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_T/(\\mathcal S_T\\cap\\mathcal N_T)\n\\]\nand preserve long exact homology sequences. By Lemma 5.1, every object of \\(\\mathcal P_T^J\\) is a summand of an object of this subcategory.</p><p>Extend a homology functor to a summand \\((X,a)\\) by taking\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}H_i(a)\n\\]\nin the abelian target. No sequence of chain projectors lifting \\(a\\) is required. Distinguished triangles in an idempotent completion are summands of distinguished triangles in the original triangulated category [1, Definition 1.10 and Theorem 1.12]. Taking images of compatible idempotents preserves exact sequences in an abelian category, so the extended functors remain homological.</p><p>Each object is a summand of a fixed bounded uniformly supported Koszul lift. Its homology vanishes outside a finite range and has lengths bounded by those of the lift. Thus all \\(b_i\\) are finite and nonnegative. The finite long exact sequences give additivity of their Euler sums.</p><p>For (4.2), the splitting (4.3) takes place in \\(\\mathcal P_T\\), because the selected powers act by zero there. Applying the extended homological functors gives its left side. The Koszul tensor has a uniformly supported lift, whose actual homology computes the right side. The identity is obtained without defining its coefficients by formal division.</p><p>Original Remark 4.1 explains why raw homology cannot be used on arbitrary lifts. Over a discrete valuation ring \\(A=T\\) with parameter \\(z\\), take \\(s_n=n\\) and the rank-one free resolutions of \\(T/(z^n)\\). The sequence lies in \\(\\mathcal N_T\\), but its raw normalized degree-zero homology length is \\(1\\). It has no uniform annihilating power before quotienting. For each fixed \\(v\\), its homology lengths after tensoring with \\(K(z^v;T)\\) are bounded independently of \\(n\\), and normalize to zero. Formula (4.2) consequently assigns zero to this quotient object, as required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The natural exact functor \\( \\mathcal S_S/(\\mathcal S_S\\cap\\mathcal N_S) \\longrightarrow \\mathcal D_S/\\mathcal N_S \\) is fully faithful.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the categories and support condition of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=A\\) or \\(T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The natural exact functor\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_S/(\\mathcal S_S\\cap\\mathcal N_S)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathcal D_S/\\mathcal N_S\n\\]\nis fully faithful. Its idempotent completion is \\(\\mathcal P_S^J\\).</p><p>Thus every supported quotient object is a summand of a uniformly supported lift, without requiring the summand projector to lift before quotienting.</p>", "proof": "<p>First let \\(X\\in\\mathcal S_S\\), \\(Y\\in\\mathcal N_S\\), and \\(f:X\\to Y\\). Choose \\(v\\) such that \\(z_j^v\\operatorname{id}_X=0\\) for every \\(j\\). Centrality gives \\(z_j^vf=0\\). The exact Hom sequence of the triangle\n\\[\n\\operatorname{fib}(z_j^v:Y\\to Y)\n\\longrightarrow Y\\xrightarrow{z_j^v}Y\n\\]\nlifts \\(f\\) through the first object. Repeat for all parameters, always using the zero action on the original source \\(X\\). The result factors \\(f\\) through\n\\[\nK_v\\otimes_A Y[-e].\n\\]\nThis object belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_S\\) by thickness and to \\(\\mathcal S_S\\) by the Koszul homotopies. Thus every map from a supported object to a small object factors through a supported small object.</p><p>A morphism between \\(X,Y\\in\\mathcal S_S\\), computed in \\(\\mathcal D_S/\\mathcal N_S\\), has a roof\n\\[\nX\\xleftarrow{s}Z\\xrightarrow{f}Y,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{cofib}(s)=N_0\\in\\mathcal N_S.\n\\]\nIn the triangle \\(Z\\to X\\xrightarrow{u}N_0\\), factor \\(u\\) through\n\\(N_1\\in\\mathcal S_S\\cap\\mathcal N_S\\) by the preceding argument. Let \\(Z_1\\) be the fiber of \\(X\\to N_1\\). The induced morphism of triangles gives\n\\[\nc:Z_1\\to Z,\n\\qquad\ns_1:Z_1\\to X,\n\\qquad\nsc=s_1.\n\\]\nNow \\(Z_1\\in\\mathcal S_S\\) and \\(\\operatorname{cofib}(s_1)=N_1\\), so\n\\[\nX\\xleftarrow{s_1}Z_1\\xrightarrow{fc}Y\n\\]\nis a roof entirely in the supported subquotient. This proves fullness.</p><p>For faithfulness, suppose a map \\(f:Z\\to Y\\) between supported objects becomes zero in \\(\\mathcal D_S/\\mathcal N_S\\). The calculus of Verdier roofs gives \\(t:W\\to Z\\) with small cone and \\(ft=0\\). The triangle of \\(t\\) makes \\(f\\) factor through that small cone. Factoring its first leg through a supported small object shows that \\(f\\) is already zero in the supported subquotient. Apply this to the numerator of a roof.</p><p>Finally, take \\(X\\in\\mathcal P_S^J\\). It is a retract of \\(q_S(Y)\\) for some \\(Y\\in\\mathcal D_S\\). Choose \\(v\\) with all \\(z_j^v\\) acting as zero on \\(X\\). The cone of each zero map splits, so \\(X\\) is a retract of \\(K_v\\otimes_A X\\). The latter is a retract of\n\\[\nq_S(K_v\\otimes_A Y),\n\\]\nwhose lift is uniformly supported. Hence \\(X\\) belongs to the idempotent completion of the supported subquotient. The reverse inclusion follows because support is closed under retracts.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(X\\in\\mathcal P_A^J\\), \\( \\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T) = r\\chi_A(X) = \\chi_C(X\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T). \\) These equalities hold for summands defined by idempotents in the Verdier quotient …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the complete setting and categories of Proposition 5: \\(A\\) is excellent regular local with maximal ideal \\(J\\), \\(T\\) is finite local domain over \\(A\\) with the same residue field, \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}_A T\\), and \\(C,\\lambda\\) satisfy (4.1).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the same \\(s_n\\) and ultrafilter \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Construct \\(\\overline{\\mathcal A}_A\\) from finite \\(A\\)-module sequences in the same way as \\(\\overline{\\mathcal A}_T\\), and let\n  \\[\n  \\chi_A:K_0(\\mathcal P_A^J)\\to\\mathbb R\n  \\]\n  be the ordinary supported Euler evaluation supplied by Proposition 5 over \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Scalar extension gives\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_A^J\\to\\mathcal P_T^J.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(X\\in\\mathcal P_A^J\\),\n\\[\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T)\n=\nr\\chi_A(X)\n=\n\\chi_C(X\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T).\n\\]\nThese equalities hold for summands defined by idempotents in the Verdier quotient, without assuming that the idempotents lift to chain maps before localization.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ordinary evaluation recovers \\(r\\) from the alternating rank of a resolution of \\(T\\); the normalized evaluation uses vanishing of positive Tor lengths. To make both calculations on an arbitrary summand, descend their finite spectral sequences through supported Verdier roofs before taking idempotent images.</p><p>Let \\(Y\\) be a uniformly supported bounded free sequence over \\(A\\), and let \\(a\\) be an idempotent endomorphism of its image in\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_A/(\\mathcal S_A\\cap\\mathcal N_A).\n\\]\nEvery supported object of \\(\\mathcal P_A\\) has this form as a summand, by Proposition 5: one may enlarge the initial lift to a bounded free sequence before taking the Koszul tensor. Put\n\\[\nV_b=\\operatorname{im}H_b^A(a)\n\\quad\\text{in }\\overline{\\mathcal A}_A.\n\\]\nThese images exist even when \\(a\\) has no chain-level projector representing it.</p><p>Choose a fixed bounded finite free \\(A\\)-resolution \\(P_\\bullet\\) of \\(T\\). Filtering \\(Y\\otimes_A P_\\bullet\\) by the degree of \\(P_\\bullet\\) gives a finite spectral sequence\n\\[\nE^1_{u,b}=H_b(Y)\\otimes_A P_u.                         \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nIt is natural in homotopy-category maps from this page onward: homotopies in \\(Y\\) preserve the filtration and act trivially on the first page. On retracts in the prequotient category, take the corresponding idempotent images on every page and on the abutment filtration. Terms and the finite abutment filtration can be regarded as \\(A\\)-modules. Ordinary \\(A\\)-length of the abutment equals its \\(T\\)-length because the residue fields agree.</p><p>A map with supported small cone is an isomorphism on \\(H_b(Y)\\) in \\(\\overline{\\mathcal A}_A\\). It is therefore an isomorphism on (4.4), on every subsequent page, and on the finite filtered abutment in the length quotient. Thus the spectral sequence descends through all supported Verdier roofs. The natural augmentation\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tot}(Y\\otimes_A P_\\bullet)\\to Y\\otimes_A T\n\\]\nidentifies its abutment with the homology of scalar extension, and this identification descends through the roofs. The induced action of \\(a\\) on the abutment is precisely the scalar-extended idempotent.</p><p>The idempotent acts on every page and on the filtered abutment, commuting with differentials. Taking its image commutes with homology, since an idempotent decomposes a complex in an abelian category into its image and complementary image. It preserves the finite exact sequences defining the filtration: if \\(F_u\\) is preserved by \\(a\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}(a)\\cap F_u=a(F_u),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{gr}_u\\operatorname{im}(a)\n=\n\\operatorname{im}(\\operatorname{gr}_u a).\n\\]\nThe Euler length on the scalar-extended summand is therefore the Euler length of the images on the first page. These are \\(\\operatorname{rank}_A P_u\\) copies of \\(V_b\\), giving\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_u(-1)^u\\operatorname{rank}_A P_u\\right)\n\\sum_b(-1)^b\\overline{\\operatorname{length}}_A V_b\n=\nr\\sum_b(-1)^b\\overline{\\operatorname{length}}_A V_b.    \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThe alternating rank is \\(r\\), by tensoring the resolution with \\(\\operatorname{Frac}(A)\\).</p><p>For \\(C\\), use the finite hyper-Tor spectral sequence\n\\[\n{}^CE^2_{u,b}\n=\n\\operatorname{Tor}_u^A(H_b(Y),C)\n\\Longrightarrow\nH_{u+b}(Y\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}C).                      \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nIt comes from the functorial finite Postnikov filtration of \\(Y\\). It has bounded \\(b\\) and \\(0\\leq u\\leq e\\), so there is no unbounded convergence issue.</p><p>Lemma 6.1 makes every positive Tor term zero in \\(\\overline{\\mathcal A}_C\\). It also makes\n\\[\nV\\longmapsto V\\otimes_A C\n\\]\nan exact functor\n\\[\n\\overline{\\mathcal A}_A\\to\\overline{\\mathcal A}_C\n\\]\nmultiplying length by \\(r\\). On a short exact sequence its possible failure of left exactness is the image of a positive Tor module of zero length. Negligible-length sequences map to negligible-length sequences by the same lemma.</p><p>A supported-small-cone map thus induces isomorphisms on the second page of (4.6) and its finite filtered abutment in the length quotient. This spectral sequence is functorial through the supported Verdier roofs. Taking the images of the same idempotent \\(a\\) leaves only the zero Tor row, namely the images of \\(V_b\\) under the exact tensor functor. Their Euler length is\n\\[\nr\\sum_b(-1)^b\\overline{\\operatorname{length}}_A V_b,\n\\]\nthe value in (4.5). The sum without \\(r\\) is \\(\\chi_A(Y,a)\\) by construction. This proves both equalities for every summand and hence for every \\(X\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_j^A(V,C)=0 \\quad(j>0), \\qquad \\lambda(V\\otimes_A C)=r\\operatorname{length}_A V. \\) All these modules are annihilated by a power of \\(J\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,J,C,\\lambda,r\\) and (4.1) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Unlike Proposition 4, \\(A\\) need not be complete.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a finite-length \\(A\\)-module.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_j^A(V,C)=0\n\\quad(j&gt;0),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda(V\\otimes_A C)=r\\operatorname{length}_A V.\n\\]\nAll these modules are annihilated by a power of \\(J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The Koszul and composition-series proof of the finite-length base-change assertion in Proposition 4 applies: it uses regularity and the length hypothesis, but not completeness. A power of \\(J\\) annihilating \\(V\\) also annihilates its derived tensor homology.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 4.7, “Asymptotic norm comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(X\\in\\mathcal P_T^J\\), \\( \\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}}(gX) = \\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_C(gX). \\) Neither invariance of \\(\\lambda\\) under \\(G\\) nor equality of the two evaluations on individual classes is assumed.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting of Proposition 5, including the excellent regular local ring \\((A,J)\\), the finite local domain \\(T\\) with the same residue field, the algebra \\(C\\), the length \\(\\lambda\\) satisfying (4.1), and the rank quotient categories defined using \\(s_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let a finite group \\(G\\) act on \\(T\\) over \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\operatorname{Spec}T\\to\\operatorname{Spec}A\\) is surjective and \\(G\\) is transitive on every geometric fiber.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(gX\\) denote scalar twisting induced by\n  \\[\n  g^*P=P\\otimes_{T,g}T.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(X\\in\\mathcal P_T^J\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}}(gX)\n=\n\\sum_{g\\in G}\\chi_C(gX).\n\\]\nNeither invariance of \\(\\lambda\\) under \\(G\\) nor equality of the two evaluations on individual classes is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 identifies the supported categories with the supported perfect categories of \\(H\\) and \\(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T\\), where \\(H\\) is connective. The identification upstairs is compatible with the \\(G\\)-action: scalar twisting acts on the \\(T\\)-factor.</p><p>Proposition 3 gives a surjection\n\\[\nK_0(\\mathcal P_A^J)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\longrightarrow\n\\bigl(K_0(\\mathcal P_T^J)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\\bigr)^G.\n\\]\nThe norm class\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in G}[gX]\n\\]\nis invariant. By Proposition 6, the homomorphism\n\\[\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{ord}}-\\chi_C,\n\\]\nextended rationally, vanishes on the image from \\(A\\). Apply it to a preimage of the norm class to obtain the asserted equality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.6, “Connective coefficients.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a connective associative derived \\(A\\)-algebra \\(H\\) and compatible equivalences \\( \\mathcal P_A\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(H), \\qquad \\mathcal P_T\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T). \\) They …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,T,s_n,\\mathcal D_S,\\mathcal N_S,\\mathcal P_S\\) from Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">All scalar localizations are along the central image of \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a connective associative derived \\(A\\)-algebra \\(H\\) and compatible equivalences\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_A\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(H),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal P_T\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T).\n\\]\nThey respect central localization by elements of \\(A\\).</p><p>Under these equivalences, the support condition defining \\(\\mathcal P_S^J\\) is central-localization support.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(S=A\\) or \\(T\\), the sequence\n\\[\nE_S=(S^{s_n})_n\n\\]\nis an additive generator. If \\(a_n=O(s_n)\\), there is a single integer \\(c\\) with \\(a_n\\leq cs_n\\) for every \\(n\\), after increasing \\(c\\) for finitely many exceptional indices. Coordinate inclusions and projections make \\((S^{a_n})_n\\) a summand of \\(E_S^c\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_S\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(B_S),\n\\qquad\nB_S=\\prod_n M_{s_n}(S).\n\\]</p><p>The \\(A\\)-module underlying \\(B_A\\) is flat. By the ideal criterion, every finitely generated ideal of the Noetherian ring \\(A\\) is finitely presented, so its tensor product with this product of finite free modules is the product of its tensor products; each required injection is a product of injections. Since \\(T\\) is finitely presented over \\(A\\), tensoring with \\(T\\) commutes with the product. Hence\n\\[\nB_A\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T\n\\simeq\nB_A\\otimes_A T\n\\simeq\nB_T.                                                   \\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p><p>The same product argument makes every morphism module between the original free sequences flat over \\(A\\). The explicit dg quotient of this degree-zero category by its full rank-small subcategory therefore computes the derived quotient [7, Section 3.1 and Theorem 3.4]. Its morphisms are sums of paths formed from tensor products of original morphism modules and contracting homotopies in homological degree \\(1\\). They are concentrated in nonnegative degrees and degreewise \\(A\\)-flat, hence are \\(K\\)-flat.</p><p>Passing to the perfect hull and idempotent completion kills exactly \\(\\mathcal N_A\\). The image of \\(E_A\\) remains a generator, and\n\\[\nH=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathcal P_A}(q_AE_A)\n\\]\nis connective. This gives \\(\\mathcal P_A\\simeq\\operatorname{Perf}(H)\\). The assertion concerns the chosen generator, not arbitrary shifts of perfect objects.</p><p>Since \\(T\\) is finitely presented over \\(A\\), tensoring each original morphism module with \\(T\\) gives the morphism module between the corresponding free \\(T\\)-sequences. Tensoring the path formula gives exactly the \\(T\\)-linear dg quotient by rank-small free sequences. The same flatness criterion applies over the Noetherian ring \\(T\\). Its endomorphism algebra is\n\\[\nH\\otimes_A T\\simeq H\\otimes_A^{\\mathbf L}T,\n\\]\nand its perfect hull and idempotent completion are \\(\\mathcal P_T\\). This agrees with preservation of exact quotients under scalar extension [18, Lemma 3.12]. The constructions are centrally \\(A\\)-linear and preserve central localization.</p><p>For a perfect module \\(X\\), localization at \\(z_j\\) is the telescope of multiplication by \\(z_j\\). Compactness implies\n\\[\nX[1/z_j]=0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nz_j^v\\operatorname{id}_X=0\n\\text{ for some }v.\n\\]\nChoose a common power for the finitely many parameters. The same argument for \\(\\operatorname{Perf}(B_S)\\) identifies \\(\\mathcal S_S\\) with the supported objects before the quotient. No uniformly supported lift of an object of \\(\\mathcal P_S^J\\) is required for this comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, together with the reduction in Lemma 5.1 and the Galois setup of Section 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The extensions \\(D'\\) and \\(E'\\) are finite local domains, have residue field \\(k\\), and have transitive geometric fibers over their respective bases.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((R,\\mathfrak m_R,k)\\) be a complete regular local ring of dimension \\(d\\), with algebraically closed residue field \\(k\\) of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  D=R/P,\\qquad E=R/Q\n  \\]\n  be domains with\n  \\[\n  h=\\dim D&gt;0,\\qquad l=\\dim E&gt;0,\\qquad h+l=d,\n  \\qquad\n  \\sqrt{P+Q}=\\mathfrak m_R.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">By Proposition 2, choose regular complete normalization bases\n  \\[\n  A_D\\subseteq D,\\qquad A_E\\subseteq E\n  \\]\n  of dimensions \\(h,l\\), with residue field \\(k\\), finite and generically separable.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D'\\) and \\(E'\\) be the integral closures of these bases in finite Galois closures of their fraction-field extensions.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  G_D=\\operatorname{Gal}(\\operatorname{Frac}(D')/\\operatorname{Frac}(A_D)),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  G_E=\\operatorname{Gal}(\\operatorname{Frac}(E')/\\operatorname{Frac}(A_E)),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  a_D=\\operatorname{rank}_D D',\n  \\qquad\n  a_E=\\operatorname{rank}_E E'.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 2 to \\(D'\\) over \\(A_D\\) and to \\(E'\\) over \\(A_E\\), and fix\n  \\[\n  (C_D,\\lambda_D)=(C_{D'},\\lambda_{D'}),\n  \\qquad\n  (C_E,\\lambda_E)=(C_{E'},\\lambda_{E'}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\sigma\\in G_D\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\iota_\\sigma:R\\to D'\\xrightarrow{\\sigma}D',\n  \\]\n  and give \\(C_D\\) the induced \\(R\\)-action. Define\n  \\[\n  c_\\sigma\n  =\n  \\sum_{i=0}^{d}(-1)^i\n  \\lambda_D\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(C_D,E')\\bigr).\n                                                               \\tag{5.2}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extensions \\(D'\\) and \\(E'\\) are finite local domains, have residue field \\(k\\), and have transitive geometric fibers over their respective bases.</p><p>Every length in (5.2) is finite, and\n\\[\n|G_D|a_Da_E\\chi^R(D,E)\n=\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in G_D}c_\\sigma.                          \\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p><p>To prove Theorem 1, it suffices to establish strict positivity for all domain pairs \\(D,E\\) satisfying the assumptions above.</p>", "proof": "<p>The reduction assertion is Lemma 8.1.</p><p>The integral closures \\(D',E'\\) are finite over their bases by excellence, and are finite over \\(D,E\\). A finite algebra over a complete local ring is a product of complete local algebras, and a domain has only one factor [28, Tag 03QH]. Thus \\(D',E'\\) are local. Their residue fields are finite extensions of the algebraically closed field \\(k\\), hence equal \\(k\\).</p><p>For either extension, write \\(T^G=A\\). This equality follows from normality of the base. Let \\(\\Omega\\) be an algebraically closed \\(A\\)-field and put\n\\[\nS=T\\otimes_A\\Omega.\n\\]\nFor \\(u=\\sum_i t_i\\otimes\\omega_i\\in S\\), the norm\n\\[\n\\prod_{g\\in G}g(u)\n\\]\nis scalar: the coefficients of\n\\[\n\\prod_{g\\in G}\\left(\\sum_i g(t_i)X_i\\right)\n\\]\nlie in \\(T^G=A\\), and one specializes \\(X_i=\\omega_i\\).</p><p>If the finite Artinian \\(\\Omega\\)-algebra \\(S\\) had more than one orbit of points, its product decomposition would give a nontrivial \\(G\\)-invariant idempotent \\(e\\), equal to one on one orbit and zero on the others. Its norm is\n\\[\ne^{|G|}=e\n\\]\nand is scalar, forcing \\(e=0\\) or \\(e=1\\), a contradiction. Thus every geometric fiber is transitive. No assertion that invariants commute with base change is used.</p><p>Take a finite \\(R\\)-free resolution of \\(E'\\) and extend it to \\(D'\\). This is a bounded free complex supported at the closed point: away from \\(\\mathfrak m_R\\), the supports of \\(D'\\) and \\(E'\\) are disjoint. The same holds after every twist, whose kernel on \\(R\\) is still \\(P\\).</p><p>Proposition 4 supplies finite normalized homology lengths and equality of ordinary and normalized Euler evaluations after summing all conjugates. Applying \\(\\sigma\\) entrywise gives a semilinear isomorphism between the untwisted complex and its conjugate. Hence all ordinary Euler characteristics in that sum equal\n\\[\n\\chi^R(D',E').\n\\]\nOrdinary lengths over \\(D'\\) and \\(R\\) agree because the residue fields are \\(k\\).</p><p>Choose an injection\n\\[\nD^{a_D}\\to D'\n\\]\nwith torsion cokernel. The cokernel has dimension less than \\(h\\), so its intersection characteristic with \\(E'\\) vanishes by (5.1). Additivity gives\n\\[\n\\chi^R(D',E')=a_D\\chi^R(D,E').\n\\]\nThe analogous argument for \\(E\\subseteq E'\\) gives\n\\[\n\\chi^R(D,E')=a_E\\chi^R(D,E).\n\\]\nThe norm comparison proves (5.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "It suffices to prove Theorem 1 for \\( D=R/P,\\qquad E=R/Q, \\) where \\(R\\) is complete with algebraically closed residue field \\(k\\) of characteristic \\(p>0\\), \\(P,Q\\) are prime …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this reduction, replace the complete-domain-pair setting of Proposition 8 by an arbitrary regular local ring and modules as in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It suffices to prove Theorem 1 for\n\\[\nD=R/P,\\qquad E=R/Q,\n\\]\nwhere \\(R\\) is complete with algebraically closed residue field \\(k\\) of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\), \\(P,Q\\) are prime,\n\\[\nh=\\dim D&gt;0,\\qquad l=\\dim E&gt;0,\\qquad h+l=\\dim R,\n\\]\nand \\(P+Q\\) is primary to \\(\\mathfrak m_R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The equicharacteristic case is Serre’s positivity theorem [25]. We may assume that the residue characteristic is positive. Completion followed by Proposition 1 reduces to a complete regular local ring with algebraically closed residue field.</p><p>A flat local extension \\(R\\to R_1\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m_RR_1=\\mathfrak m_{R_1}\n\\]\npreserves the length of finite-length modules: a composition series becomes a filtration with factors \\(R_1/\\mathfrak m_{R_1}\\). Flat base change identifies Tor modules after extension. Applied to \\(M/\\mathfrak m_R^nM\\), the same argument preserves the Hilbert–Samuel function and hence dimension; likewise for \\(N\\). Regularity is preserved because dimension and the minimal number of maximal-ideal generators are preserved.</p><p>Use the Roberts–Gillet–Soulé vanishing theorem in its general regular local form:\n\\[\n\\chi^R(V,W)=0\n\\quad\\text{if}\\quad\n\\operatorname{length}_R(V\\otimes_R W)&lt;\\infty,\n\\quad\n\\dim V+\\dim W&lt;d.                                      \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nTake prime filtrations of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Every pair of factors still has finite-length tensor product because its supports lie in the original supports. Additivity and (5.1) discard every pair except those having both original dimensions. There are top-dimensional factors in both filtrations, so at least one pair remains. Strict positivity for every remaining pair proves strict positivity for \\(M,N\\). Their primes satisfy\n\\[\n\\sqrt{P+Q}=\\mathfrak m_R.\n\\]</p><p>A zero-dimensional prime quotient is \\(k\\), and its complementary \\(d\\)-dimensional prime quotient is \\(R\\), since a regular local ring is a domain. For that pair the characteristic is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_R k=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(h,l&gt;0\\) may be assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, with the parameter choice of Section 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Such a sequence \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_h\\) exists.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the complete regular local ring \\(R\\), the domain pair \\(D=R/P,E=R/Q\\), the dimensions \\(h,l\\), the extensions \\(D',E'\\), and the fixed length algebras of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\sigma\\in G_D\\). Write\n  \\[\n  C=C_D,\\qquad \\lambda=\\lambda_D,\n  \\]\n  with \\(R\\)-action through \\(\\iota_\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose\n  \\[\n  x_1,\\ldots,x_h\\in Q                               \\tag{5.4}\n  \\]\n  forming an \\(R\\)-regular sequence whose images form a parameter system of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B=R/(x_1,\\ldots,x_h),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  I_\\sigma=(\\iota_\\sigma(x_1),\\ldots,\\iota_\\sigma(x_h))D',\n  \\qquad\n  U=C/I_\\sigma C.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Such a sequence \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_h\\) exists. The ideal \\(I_\\sigma\\) is a parameter ideal of \\(D'\\), and\n\\[\n0&lt;\\lambda(U)=e(I_\\sigma,D')&lt;\\infty,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda H_q(x;C)=0\\quad(q&gt;0).                         \\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThere is an integer \\(a\\geq1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m_B^a(D'/I_\\sigma)=0,                       \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nand hence \\(\\mathfrak m_B^a\\) annihilates \\(U\\) and every \\(D'/I_\\sigma\\)-module.</p><p>For every finite \\(B\\)-module \\(L\\), all lengths below are finite, and\n\\[\n\\tau_i(L)\n:=\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(U,L)\n=\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(C,L),\n\\qquad\n\\tau_i(L)=0\\quad(i&gt;l).                               \\tag{5.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the simultaneous parameter and regular-sequence choice, suppose \\(i-1\\) elements have been selected. The associated primes of\n\\[\nR/(x_1,\\ldots,x_{i-1})\n\\]\nhave height \\(i-1\\), since \\(R\\) is Cohen–Macaulay. None contains \\(Q\\), whose height is \\(h\\geq i\\).</p><p>Also avoid the primes in \\(D/(x_1,\\ldots,x_{i-1})\\) obstructing the next parameter dimension drop. Each has positive dimension. If its inverse image contained \\(Q\\), it would contain \\(P+Q\\) and hence be the maximal ideal, a contradiction. Prime avoidance inside \\(Q\\) makes both choices possible at every step.</p><p>Finiteness and then the automorphism \\(\\sigma\\) preserve the parameter condition, so \\(I_\\sigma\\) is a parameter ideal of \\(D'\\). Proposition 2 gives (5.5). Since \\(D'/I_\\sigma\\) is Artinian, some \\(a\\geq1\\) gives (5.6).</p><p>For the Tor comparison, the complexes have compatible \\(C\\)-actions. The Koszul homology \\(H_q(x;C)\\) is annihilated by \\(x\\) and \\(P\\), hence has bounded primary torsion. For \\(q&gt;0\\), its length is zero by (5.5). A \\(B\\)-free resolution of \\(L\\), finite in each degree, gives\n\\[\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_p^B(H_q(x;C),L)=0\n\\quad(q&gt;0),\n\\]\nbecause the homology is a subquotient of a finite direct sum of a length-zero module. For \\(q=0\\), the lengths are finite because \\(\\lambda(U)&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>The change-of-rings spectral sequence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_p^B\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tor}_q^R(C,B),L\\bigr)\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Tor}_{p+q}^R(C,L)\n\\]\ngives the equality of lengths in (5.7). Only finitely many terms enter each total degree.</p><p>For the sharper bound, choose an \\(R\\)-free resolution \\(P_L\\) of \\(L\\) in degrees \\(0,\\ldots,d\\), and consider\n\\[\nK(x;R)\\otimes_R C\\otimes_R P_L.\n\\]\nTaking Koszul homology first, all positive rows have length zero, and the zero row is \\(U\\otimes_R P_L\\). Thus total homology has length zero in degrees greater than \\(d\\).</p><p>On the other hand, \\(xL=0\\), so\n\\[\nK(x;R)\\otimes_R^{\\mathbf L}L\n\\simeq\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{h}L[j]^{\\oplus\\binom hj}.\n\\]\nTensoring derivedly with \\(C\\) preserves this splitting as a statement about \\(C\\)-complexes. Total homology in degree \\(i+h\\) contains\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(C,L)\n\\]\nas a direct summand. If \\(i&gt;l=d-h\\), this total degree exceeds \\(d\\), proving the bound. The argument also shows finiteness of all the displayed lengths.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are finite-length \\(B\\)-modules \\(M_n\\) and positive integers \\(s_n\\to\\infty\\), with \\( \\mathfrak m_B^aM_n=0, \\) such that \\( \\frac{\\operatorname{length}_B M_n}{s_n} \\longrightarrow\\lambda(U), \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed \\(\\sigma\\), the rings \\(B,D',E'\\), the ideal \\(I_\\sigma\\), the module \\(U\\), the integer \\(a\\), and the functions \\(\\lambda,\\tau_i\\) from Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A=A_D\\), with tower \\(A_j\\) and union \\(\\Lambda\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  s(j)=\\operatorname{rank}_A A_j=p^{jh},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  S_j=A_j\\otimes_A(D'/I_\\sigma),\n  \\qquad\n  S=\\Lambda\\otimes_A(D'/I_\\sigma).                    \\tag{5.8}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The commuting \\(\\Lambda\\)- and \\(D'/I_\\sigma\\)-actions make \\(U\\) an \\(S\\)-module. The \\(B\\)-action is induced by\n  \\[\n  B\\to D'/I_\\sigma\n  \\]\n  through \\(\\iota_\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In this proposition, \\(\\lambda\\) is the tower length on underlying modules, without requiring a \\(C\\)-action.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are finite-length \\(B\\)-modules \\(M_n\\) and positive integers \\(s_n\\to\\infty\\), with\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m_B^aM_n=0,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{length}_B M_n}{s_n}\n\\longrightarrow\\lambda(U),                           \\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{length}_B\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(M_n,L)}{s_n}\n\\longrightarrow\\tau_i(L)\n\\qquad\n(L=k,E',\\ i\\geq0).                                   \\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n\\beta_0(M_n)=\\dim_k(M_n/\\mathfrak m_BM_n),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\frac{\\beta_0(M_n)}{s_n}\\longrightarrow\\nu_\\sigma,\n\\qquad\n\\nu_\\sigma\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\lambda(U)}{\\operatorname{length}_B(B/\\mathfrak m_B^a)}\n&gt;0.                                                   \\tag{5.11}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The algebra \\(S\\) is finitely presented as a \\(\\Lambda\\)-module and has finite normalized length. Every \\(S_j\\) is finite over the twisted image of \\(B\\): \\(D'\\) is finite over the twisted image of \\(R/P\\), and \\(A_j\\) is finite free over \\(A\\). Equation (5.6) annihilates \\(S_j\\) and \\(S\\).</p><p>Each \\(S_j\\) is Artinian local with residue field \\(k\\). The tower parameter roots are nilpotent over the Artinian quotient \\(D'/I_\\sigma\\), and reduction leaves the common residue field \\(k\\). Thus finite \\(S_j\\)-modules have finite lengths over both \\(B\\) and \\(A_j\\), and these lengths agree: a composition series over \\(S_j\\) has factors \\(k\\), of length one for either ring. This remains true if a residue-field identification is composed with an automorphism.</p><p>Given \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), choose a finitely generated \\(S\\)-submodule\n\\[\nV\\subseteq U\n\\]\nwith \\(\\lambda(U/V)&lt;\\epsilon\\). This follows from the supremum property: the \\(S\\)-span of a finitely generated \\(\\Lambda\\)-submodule is still finitely generated over \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p>Choose a surjection \\(S^r\\to V\\) with kernel \\(K\\). The kernel has finite length bounded by \\(r\\lambda(S)\\). Choose a finitely generated \\(S\\)-submodule \\(K_0\\subseteq K\\) with\n\\[\n\\lambda(K/K_0)&lt;\\epsilon.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nP_\\epsilon=S^r/K_0\\longrightarrow U\n\\]\nhas kernel and cokernel of length less than \\(\\epsilon\\). It is finitely presented over \\(S\\) and \\(\\Lambda\\), and remains annihilated by \\(\\mathfrak m_B^a\\).</p><p>Fix \\(B\\)-resolutions of \\(k\\) and \\(E'\\) with finite free terms, and write \\(r_i(L)\\) for the rank in degree \\(i\\), with \\(r_{-1}(L)=0\\). If \\(K_\\epsilon,W_\\epsilon\\) are the kernel and cokernel of \\(P_\\epsilon\\to U\\), the two long exact homology sequences give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_\\epsilon,L)\n-\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(U,L)\n\\right|\n\\leq{}&amp;\n(r_i(L)+r_{i-1}(L))\\lambda(K_\\epsilon)\\\\\n&amp;+\n(r_{i+1}(L)+r_i(L))\\lambda(W_\\epsilon).\n\\end{aligned}                                                   \\qquad(5.12)\n\\]\nFor example, homology after tensoring the resolution with \\(K_\\epsilon\\) has length bounded by the corresponding rank times \\(\\lambda(K_\\epsilon)\\); likewise for \\(W_\\epsilon\\). All quantities are finite. Also\n\\[\n|\\lambda(P_\\epsilon)-\\lambda(U)|&lt;2\\epsilon.\n\\]</p><p>A finite \\(S\\)-presentation of \\(P_\\epsilon\\) descends to an \\(S_j\\)-module \\(P_j\\) for some \\(j\\), with\n\\[\nP_\\epsilon=\\Lambda\\otimes_{A_j}P_j.\n\\]\nFlatness gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_\\epsilon,L)\n=\n\\Lambda\\otimes_{A_j}\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_j,L).\n\\]\nThe finite-stage homology is finite over \\(S_j\\). The normalization formula and the length comparison give exactly\n\\[\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_\\epsilon,L)\n=\n\\frac{\\operatorname{length}_{A_j}\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_j,L)}{s(j)}\n=\n\\frac{\\operatorname{length}_B\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(P_j,L)}{s(j)}.\n\\]\nThe same formula holds for \\(P_\\epsilon\\).</p><p>At step \\(n\\), choose \\(\\epsilon\\) so that all errors in (5.12), for \\(i\\leq n\\) and \\(L=k,E'\\), and the length error are less than \\(1/n\\). Descend the presentation and enlarge the stage so that \\(j\\geq n\\). Further flat tower extension leaves normalized values unchanged. Take\n\\[\nM_n=P_j,\\qquad s_n=s(j).\n\\]\nThis proves (5.9), (5.10), the common annihilator, and \\(s_n\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>The case \\(i=0,L=k\\) gives the limit in (5.11). A minimal generating set gives a surjection\n\\[\n(B/\\mathfrak m_B^a)^{\\beta_0(M_n)}\\to M_n.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_B M_n\n\\leq\n\\beta_0(M_n)\\operatorname{length}_B(B/\\mathfrak m_B^a).\n\\]\nDivide by \\(s_n\\) and take limits to obtain the positive lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, with the construction of Section 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "In each fixed degree, \\( \\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}=O(s_n), \\qquad \\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}=o(s_n) \\quad(i>l). \\) For every \\(\\gamma\\in G_E\\), the sequence \\( (F'_{n,\\gamma})_n \\) is supported at …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed \\(\\sigma\\), the rings and length algebras of Propositions 8 and 9, and the modules \\(M_n\\) and integers \\(s_n\\) of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose minimal free \\(B\\)-resolutions \\(F_n^\\infty\\) of \\(M_n\\), homologically indexed in nonnegative degrees.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_n\\) be their brutal truncations to degrees \\(0,\\ldots,l\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_l\\) be regular parameters of \\(A_E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\gamma\\in G_E\\), extend \\(F_n\\) through\n  \\[\n  B\\to E\\to E'\\xrightarrow{\\gamma}E',\n  \\]\n  and denote the resulting complex by \\(F'_{n,\\gamma}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the scale \\(s_n\\) in the asymptotic category over \\(E'\\), with base \\(A_E\\), as defined in Proposition 5. No identification of the \\(A_D\\)- and \\(A_E\\)-towers is assumed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In each fixed degree,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}=O(s_n),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}=o(s_n)\n\\quad(i&gt;l).                                           \\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\gamma\\in G_E\\), the sequence\n\\[\n(F'_{n,\\gamma})_n\n\\]\nis supported at the closed point in the asymptotic category. Its ordinary Euler evaluation equals \\(c_\\sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Minimality and Proposition 10 give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}\n=\n\\operatorname{length}_B\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(M_n,k).\n\\]\nProposition 9 gives \\(\\tau_i(k)=0\\) for \\(i&gt;l\\), so (5.13) follows.</p><p>Every \\(b\\in\\mathfrak m_B^a\\) acts nullhomotopically on \\(F_n^\\infty\\), since it annihilates \\(M_n\\). Restrict a chosen homotopy to the brutal truncation. Its only remaining error is in degree \\(l\\), where it is\n\\[\nF_{n,l}\n\\xrightarrow{h_l}\nF^\\infty_{n,l+1}\n\\xrightarrow{d_{l+1}}\nF_{n,l}.                                               \\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nThis factors by chain maps through \\(F^\\infty_{n,l+1}[l]\\): the map induced by \\(d_{l+1}\\) is a chain map because \\(d_ld_{l+1}=0\\), and the map induced by \\(h_l\\) is a chain map because the truncation has no degree \\(l+1\\) term. The middle module has rank \\(o(s_n)\\), hence is killed in the asymptotic quotient. These identities persist after extension to \\(E'\\), without any flatness assumption over \\(B\\).</p><p>For each structural map, \\(\\mathfrak m_BE'\\) is primary to \\(\\mathfrak m_{E'}\\). Choose \\(v\\) large enough that every \\(z_j^v\\) lies in the extension of \\(\\mathfrak m_B^a\\) for all \\(\\gamma\\); one fixed choice suffices because \\(G_E\\) is finite. Expressing \\(z_j^v\\) as a finite \\(E'\\)-linear combination of elements of \\(\\mathfrak m_B^a\\) proves that it acts by zero on the sequence in the quotient. The same combinations give genuine nullhomotopies on the full extended resolutions. This proves support.</p><p>Apply the ordinary form of (4.2) from Proposition 5. In its right side, one may replace truncated extended resolutions by full ones in each fixed homological degree. The quotient of the full resolution by the brutal truncation has terms only in degrees at least \\(l+1\\), all of rank \\(o(s_n)\\) in each fixed degree. After tensoring the tail with \\(K(z^v;E')\\), taking Koszul homology first bounds each fixed-degree homology by finite sums of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_B F^\\infty_{n,i}\\,\n\\operatorname{length}_{E'}H_q(z^v;E')\n=\no(s_n).\n\\]\nThe Koszul homology lengths are fixed and finite, and only finitely many terms contribute to each total degree. The long exact homology sequence proves the replacement assertion.</p><p>On each full extended resolution, the powers \\(z_j^v\\) act nullhomotopically. Koszul tensoring gives the direct sum of shifts with multiplicities \\(\\binom lj\\). Its underlying homology has ordinary lengths\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_B\\operatorname{Tor}_i^B(M_n,E'),\n\\]\nunchanged by conjugating the \\(E'\\)-action: applying \\(\\gamma\\) entrywise is a semilinear isomorphism.</p><p>Propositions 10 and 9, followed by coefficient comparison in (4.2), identify the ordinary homology-length coefficients with\n\\[\n\\tau_i(E')\n=\n\\lambda\\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(C,E').\n\\]\nTheir alternating sum is \\(c_\\sigma\\), as defined in Proposition 8.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 5.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( 0<\\eta_E<\\infty, \\qquad r_E\\leq t_E\\eta_E, \\) and the \\(\\lambda_E\\)-homology-length coefficients of each supported sequence vanish outside degree zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed \\(\\sigma\\), the supported sequences \\((F'_{n,\\gamma})_n\\), and all data of Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Evaluate these sequences using the fixed algebra \\(C_E\\) and length \\(\\lambda_E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\eta_E=\\lambda_E(C_E/\\mathfrak m_{E'}C_E),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak q_E=(z_1,\\ldots,z_l)E',\n  \\qquad\n  r_E=\\operatorname{rank}_{A_E}E',\n  \\qquad\n  t_E=\\operatorname{length}_{E'}(E'/\\mathfrak q_E).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b_{\\sigma,\\gamma}\\) be the \\(\\lambda_E\\)-Euler evaluation of \\((F'_{n,\\gamma})_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu_\\sigma\\) be the limit from Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n0&lt;\\eta_E&lt;\\infty,\n\\qquad\nr_E\\leq t_E\\eta_E,                                    \\tag{5.15--5.16}\n\\]\nand the \\(\\lambda_E\\)-homology-length coefficients of each supported sequence vanish outside degree zero.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nb_{\\sigma,\\gamma}\n\\geq\n\\nu_\\sigma\\eta_E\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\lambda_D(U)}\n{\\operatorname{length}_B(B/\\mathfrak m_B^a)}\n\\frac{r_E}{t_E}\n&gt;0.                                                    \\tag{5.17}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n0&lt;\\eta_E=\\lambda_E(C_E/\\mathfrak m_{E'}C_E).\n\\]\nThe base-parameter length identity gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_E(C_E/\\mathfrak q_EC_E)=r_E&gt;0.\n\\]\nTensor a composition series of \\(E'/\\mathfrak q_E\\) right-exactly with \\(C_E\\). At each step, the image of the tensor of the submodule has length at most that tensor’s length; injectivity after tensoring is not required. Thus\n\\[\nr_E\\leq t_E\\eta_E.\n\\]\nThe surjection\n\\[\nC_E/\\mathfrak q_EC_E\n\\to\nC_E/\\mathfrak m_{E'}C_E\n\\]\ngives finiteness of \\(\\eta_E\\).</p><p>Let \\(b_i\\) be the nonnegative homology-length coefficients of the \\(\\lambda_E\\)-evaluation in (4.2). Consider\n\\[\nK(z^v;E')\\otimes_{E'}F'_{n,\\gamma}\\otimes_{E'}C_E.\n\\]\nTaking Koszul homology first, Proposition 2 makes all positive Koszul rows have length zero. The remaining row has complex degrees \\(0,\\ldots,l\\). Hence normalized homology lengths of the double complex vanish in total degrees greater than \\(l\\), as well as in negative degrees.</p><p>Equation (4.2) says their polynomial is\n\\[\n(1+t)^l\\sum_i b_it^i.\n\\]\nEvery \\(b_i\\) is nonnegative. Boundedness and comparison of the least degree rule out negative-degree coefficients. If \\(b_i&gt;0\\) for some \\(i&gt;0\\), there would be a positive coefficient in degree \\(i+l&gt;l\\), a contradiction. Only \\(b_0\\) can be nonzero, so the Euler evaluation equals \\(b_0\\).</p><p>In degree zero, the double complex has homology\n\\[\nC_E^{\\beta_0(M_n)}\n\\Big/\n\\left(\n\\operatorname{im}(d_1)\n+\n(z_1^v,\\ldots,z_l^v)C_E^{\\beta_0(M_n)}\n\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(l&gt;0\\), \\(d_1\\) is the degree-one presentation differential of \\(M_n\\). Minimality over \\(B\\) puts its entries in \\(\\mathfrak m_B\\), whose image under every twist lies in \\(\\mathfrak m_{E'}\\). The base parameter powers also lie in \\(\\mathfrak m_{E'}\\). There is therefore a surjection from the displayed homology onto\n\\[\n(C_E/\\mathfrak m_{E'}C_E)^{\\beta_0(M_n)}.\n\\]\nIts normalized length is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{\\beta_0(M_n)\\eta_E}{s_n}.\n\\]\nThe degree-zero coefficient in (4.2) is \\(b_0\\). Taking the ultralimit and using the ordinary limit from Proposition 10 gives\n\\[\nb_0\\geq\\nu_\\sigma\\eta_E.\n\\]\nThe lower bound for \\(\\nu_\\sigma\\) in Proposition 10 and \\(r_E\\leq t_E\\eta_E\\) give (5.17). Set \\(b_{\\sigma,\\gamma}=b_0\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 20, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [3, 21, "cite"], [3, 22, "cite"], [3, 24, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 17, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 23, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"]]});
